{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact clean head 7a260ce94dc988590fcb0fd0c6cacc486fdfdd3c, against review request git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2c416091490ef151121e63edc57ae408ffc9b627 and promise git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:02275e46e40a7b298d4f317561169fd22d0ad308.\n\n1. The fold cannot infer the proposed meaning from rests_on. The new world-staleness bit assumes that citing a retired artifact means a statement describes that artifact's behaviour, but the same edge also records evidence and reasoning. This produces the false positive the branch's own author recorded: using a binary built from this exact head against the live workroom, the current world-basis artifact for 7a260ce is itself rendered “STALE — describes a superseded world” because its justification chain mentions the predecessor artifact that it correctly superseded. Current workflow-guard and checkpoint artifacts are similarly marked. The implementation therefore makes healthy current work claim that its described world moved. Separate the descriptive dependency from general reasoning/provenance, or ratify another model that has both a real-log must-flare and must-not-flare control, before implementing this convention.\n\n2. The second commit's path aggregation is not an actionable situation count and does not solve the rendering noise it cites. For artifacts A, B and C at one path with no supersessions, B's live predecessor A and C's live predecessor B are two distinct omitted acts under the normative rule; superseding A clears B's warning but cannot clear C's. Counting one distinct path calls two required repairs one situation. The new test encodes this undercount by treating three missing adjacent supersessions across two paths as “2 paths.” The human table also still prints every per-row warning, so the measured 56 warnings remain visible; only an aggregate sentence was added. Keep exact per-artifact facts in JSON if desired, but make the human projection identify each actionable missing transition or group a path with an honest count.\n\n3. unable_to_flare covers only an empty rests_on list, not an unresolvable one. A nonempty dangling basis projects current, cannot become an effective earlier basis later, and cannot be effectively retired, yet this code reports UnableToFlare=false. The review request specifically called out that existing behavior. Either validate cited bases at admission or mark artifacts whose cited bases cannot participate in staleness; add a positive dangling-basis test.\n\nBaseline gates on the exact head passed: go vet, the full Go test suite, the full race suite, and diff check. I independently applied build-valid mutations that disabled world propagation, inverted artifact-kind discrimination, inverted the succession predicate, and inverted empty-basis detection; each made its named test fail. One earlier artifact-kind mutation was compile-invalid and was discarded before the build-valid probe. No admission or kernel rule changed. The exact-path and missing-reference-documentation limits were confirmed as stated, but the three findings above prevent approval. No merge performed.","body":{"branch":"task/world-basis","head":"7a260ce94dc988590fcb0fd0c6cacc486fdfdd3c","request":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2c416091490ef151121e63edc57ae408ffc9b627","tests":"clean detached exact head; vet; full test; full race; diff check; exact-head binary against live depth-646 workroom; four build-valid mutations","verdict":"changes-requested"}}